WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.329 --> 00:00:03.560
Here’s a question for you, SCP Explained
fans.

00:00:03.560 --> 00:00:08.540
What do the following items have in common:
A romance novel about long-dead KFC Founder

00:00:08.540 --> 00:00:14.690
Colonel Harland Sanders, a 27-pound bucket
of mac and cheese, radioactive uranium ore,

00:00:14.690 --> 00:00:18.660
and a wallet that looks like a pair of tighty-whities
with skid marks.

00:00:18.660 --> 00:00:21.220
You can buy them all on Amazon.

00:00:21.220 --> 00:00:22.220
Amazon.

00:00:22.220 --> 00:00:27.400
It’s one of the world’s largest online
retailers, famous for its wide range of products

00:00:27.400 --> 00:00:32.550
and services, its generally pretty reliable
delivery, and its appalling treatment of its

00:00:32.550 --> 00:00:33.550
workers.

00:00:33.550 --> 00:00:36.630
It’s also really, really weird.

00:00:36.630 --> 00:00:40.380
Seeing as almost anyone can set up a store
on Amazon, and the fact it’s one of the

00:00:40.380 --> 00:00:45.500
most popular storefronts in the world, it’s
natural that a huge number of lovable nutcases

00:00:45.500 --> 00:00:48.820
would set up shop and ply their super bizarre
trade.

00:00:48.820 --> 00:00:53.980
There’s such a breadth of weird items you
can buy on Amazon that it seems almost…

00:00:53.980 --> 00:00:54.980
Anomalous.

00:00:54.980 --> 00:00:59.570
And nobody knows this better than one of the
SCP Foundation’s greatest and most eccentric

00:00:59.570 --> 00:01:02.430
researchers, Dr. Jack Bright.

00:01:02.430 --> 00:01:05.110
What does Dr. Bright have to do with weird
Amazon purchases?

00:01:05.110 --> 00:01:11.020
It’s a hell of a story, and it begins, like
many stories at the SCP Foundation do, with

00:01:11.020 --> 00:01:18.090
SCP-682, the Hard to Destroy Reptile, breaching
containment and killing a hell of a lot of

00:01:18.090 --> 00:01:19.090
people.

00:01:19.090 --> 00:01:23.020
This latest containment breach actually comes
as a result of what one might professionally

00:01:23.020 --> 00:01:28.110
call “a major booboo” by Junior Researcher
Eliot Hayberg.

00:01:28.110 --> 00:01:32.670
Sadly for young Dr. Hayberg, this actually
happened on the first day of his new research

00:01:32.670 --> 00:01:38.700
assignment on the SCP-682 termination project
- Which seemed about as likely to bear fruit

00:01:38.700 --> 00:01:43.930
as attempts to find pots full of leprechaun
gold at the end of nearby rainbows, of course,

00:01:43.930 --> 00:01:48.540
carried out by MTF Omega-420, also known as
“The Luck of the Irish.”

00:01:48.540 --> 00:01:50.740
But that’s another video entirely.

00:01:50.740 --> 00:01:55.350
Needless to say, as he helped the janitorial
staff mop up all the blood while a group of

00:01:55.350 --> 00:02:00.820
guards carried a heavily injured 682 back
to its cell, Dr. Hayberg felt pretty bummed

00:02:00.820 --> 00:02:02.700
about this whole situation.

00:02:02.700 --> 00:02:07.220
Of course, the loss of life caused by Hayberg’s
cross-test was a tragedy, but he also felt

00:02:07.220 --> 00:02:10.619
terrible for letting his bosses down on his
first day.

00:02:10.619 --> 00:02:15.190
Aside from quite literally dying or being
given a fate worse than death by some horrifying

00:02:15.190 --> 00:02:19.980
anomaly, this was really the worst way to
start a first day at the SCP Foundation.

00:02:19.980 --> 00:02:25.300
Dr. Bright, a Foundation veteran by anyone’s
definition, noticed that Dr. Hayberg was looking

00:02:25.300 --> 00:02:30.530
upset and decided to go and offer some sage
advice - While also taking an opportunity

00:02:30.530 --> 00:02:33.140
to tell one of his trademark tall tales.

00:02:33.140 --> 00:02:34.480
Was it a true story?

00:02:34.480 --> 00:02:38.610
Who knows, but it was certainly true that
it was a story.

00:02:38.610 --> 00:02:43.650
“Don’t beat yourself up,” Dr. Bright
said, giving Dr. Hayberg a comforting, fatherly

00:02:43.650 --> 00:02:45.060
pat on the shoulder.

00:02:45.060 --> 00:02:47.730
“682 gets out and kills a bunch of people
every month.

00:02:47.730 --> 00:02:48.730
It’s no biggie.

00:02:48.730 --> 00:02:53.660
I’ve made way bigger mistakes than this
- compared to the Amazon Incident, this is

00:02:53.660 --> 00:02:54.660
nothing.”

00:02:54.660 --> 00:02:58.680
The advice immediately made Dr. Hayberg feel
a little better, but he was also immediately

00:02:58.680 --> 00:03:01.459
curious, replying: “The Amazon Incident?

00:03:01.459 --> 00:03:04.160
I can’t say I’m familiar with that…”

00:03:04.160 --> 00:03:08.040
Almost as though Dr. Bright had only comforted
Dr. Hayberg in the first place to give himself

00:03:08.040 --> 00:03:12.770
an opportunity to tell his story, he sat the
Junior Researcher down and immediately began

00:03:12.770 --> 00:03:14.080
to spin his yarn…

00:03:14.080 --> 00:03:19.520
A long time ago, in a galaxy far, far away,
Dr. Bright was having one of his rare slow

00:03:19.520 --> 00:03:20.540
days.

00:03:20.540 --> 00:03:25.020
He sat around his office, occasionally spinning
around on his office chair and contemplating

00:03:25.020 --> 00:03:27.569
life, the universe, and everything.

00:03:27.569 --> 00:03:32.120
The funny thing about being immortal is that
it makes your time utterly worthless.

00:03:32.120 --> 00:03:36.360
Death is a fantastic motivator because it
means that no matter how many times you say

00:03:36.360 --> 00:03:40.410
to yourself, “Ehhh, I’ll do it later”,
you know that someday there’ll be no more

00:03:40.410 --> 00:03:41.410
later.

00:03:41.410 --> 00:03:44.200
It starts a fire under you and gets you moving.

00:03:44.200 --> 00:03:49.760
But when you’re immortal like Dr. Bright,
thanks to his SCP-963 medallion, there’s

00:03:49.760 --> 00:03:54.590
always going to be a “later” to foist
your problems onto until the sun expands and

00:03:54.590 --> 00:03:57.200
devours the earth in billions of years' time.

00:03:57.200 --> 00:04:01.819
Today, however, Dr. Bright decided to spend
his time looking into the rates of containment

00:04:01.819 --> 00:04:06.950
breaches at Site-19, and pondering how exactly
he could reduce these numbers.

00:04:06.950 --> 00:04:12.370
Site-19 had impeccable security and a wide
range of elite containment specialists, but

00:04:12.370 --> 00:04:16.709
while these factors can help address containment
breaches when they happen, Dr. Bright wanted

00:04:16.709 --> 00:04:21.409
to instead treat the cause of containment
breaches rather than treating them symptomatically.

00:04:21.409 --> 00:04:26.800
In other words, rather than preventing successful
breaches from anomalies, Dr. Bright wanted

00:04:26.800 --> 00:04:32.080
to figure out how to stop anomalies from wanting
to breach containment in the first place.

00:04:32.080 --> 00:04:34.520
That’s when he had a brainwave.

00:04:34.520 --> 00:04:39.499
A few weeks earlier, he’d read an article
about the revolutionary Norwegian prison system,

00:04:39.499 --> 00:04:44.090
which used a high quality of life in prison
with a great degree of inmate enrichment to

00:04:44.090 --> 00:04:46.069
slash recidivism rates.

00:04:46.069 --> 00:04:51.580
This, to Dr. Bright, seemed like a fascinating
idea: If he gave a bunch of the more problematic

00:04:51.580 --> 00:04:56.319
sentient and sapient anomalies better enrichment,
then perhaps they’d be too busy enjoying

00:04:56.319 --> 00:04:59.809
themselves to want to break out and cause
chaos elsewhere.

00:04:59.809 --> 00:05:04.009
And Dr. Bright had an ingenious way of finding
the right enrichment for the many anomalies

00:05:04.009 --> 00:05:09.979
under Site-19’s care, and even beyond: He’d
give them computers or smartphones with fixed

00:05:09.979 --> 00:05:15.360
access to Amazon.com, where they could build
up their wish lists and give him a starting

00:05:15.360 --> 00:05:20.229
point for what kind of objects and activities
might be ideal enrichment for each of the

00:05:20.229 --> 00:05:21.460
anomalies in question.

00:05:21.460 --> 00:05:25.479
As far as Dr. Bright had figured, there would
be no way that this new plan could possibly

00:05:25.479 --> 00:05:26.550
go wrong.

00:05:26.550 --> 00:05:29.080
All he needed to do was get started.

00:05:29.080 --> 00:05:33.919
So, over the next several days, Dr. Bright
devoted himself to giving a cavalcade of anomalies

00:05:33.919 --> 00:05:39.110
devices locked on the Amazon app, and told
them to spend some time browsing, adding any

00:05:39.110 --> 00:05:43.809
items that they thought they might like to
the wishlist for later consideration.

00:05:43.809 --> 00:05:49.699
It was the exact kind of deranged genius that
only a maverick like Jack Bright could muster,

00:05:49.699 --> 00:05:54.310
and in the days and hours that followed, reams
of fascinating data rolled in.

00:05:54.310 --> 00:05:58.560
Huge numbers of anomalies picked incredibly
bizarre products for their wish lists, and

00:05:58.560 --> 00:06:03.090
in seeing which anomalies picked, he got an
unprecedented look into the minds of some

00:06:03.090 --> 00:06:05.620
of the Foundation’s most infamous inmates.

00:06:05.620 --> 00:06:09.759
And of course, we’re gonna tell you about
some of the craziest ones.

00:06:09.759 --> 00:06:13.979
What kind of monsters would we be to play
those cards close to our chest?

00:06:13.979 --> 00:06:17.430
These are some of the weirdest Amazon products
that the different anomalies added to their

00:06:17.430 --> 00:06:22.349
wishlists - And we’re sorry to inform you,
it’s gonna get even wilder after that.

00:06:22.349 --> 00:06:27.719
First, to bring things full circle, there’s
SCP-682 - who was temporarily dragged out

00:06:27.719 --> 00:06:31.789
of the acid tank he’s permanently melting
in and given an iPhone.

00:06:31.789 --> 00:06:34.819
After eating the first three iPhones, and
throwing the next several at guards at such

00:06:34.819 --> 00:06:40.199
incredible speeds that it caused considerable
injuries, he finally begrudgingly agreed to

00:06:40.199 --> 00:06:41.529
play along.

00:06:41.529 --> 00:06:47.230
After scrolling for a considerable amount
of time, 682 added bacon strip bandages, which

00:06:47.230 --> 00:06:51.099
he intended to gift to guards and researchers
to make them taste better.

00:06:51.099 --> 00:06:55.030
He also added a taco sleeping bag for the
exact same reasons.

00:06:55.030 --> 00:07:00.610
And the exotic meats beef jerky, because honestly,
Foundation staff and dead cows get a little

00:07:00.610 --> 00:07:02.520
samey after a while.

00:07:02.520 --> 00:07:07.149
And yes, before you ask, everything featured
in this video is an incredibly weird, real

00:07:07.149 --> 00:07:08.539
Amazon product.

00:07:08.539 --> 00:07:10.789
We honestly kind of wish we were kidding.

00:07:10.789 --> 00:07:15.830
Next, Dr. Bright started collecting the data
from SCP-106’s wishlist.

00:07:15.830 --> 00:07:20.969
The deranged and sadistic Old Man seemed to
enjoy nothing but doling out pain to his victims,

00:07:20.969 --> 00:07:25.419
but given the seemingly limitless purview
of Amazon, he went a little nuts.

00:07:25.419 --> 00:07:29.869
First, he added the Hatch Your Own Spiders
Terrarium, perhaps believing that filling

00:07:29.869 --> 00:07:34.649
his pocket dimension with thousands of spiders
would make it an even more delightfully miserable

00:07:34.649 --> 00:07:36.289
place for his victims.

00:07:36.289 --> 00:07:41.870
As a delicious snack fit for an evil old anomalous
lunatic, he decided to add some Canned Edible

00:07:41.870 --> 00:07:43.749
Smoked Rattlesnake.

00:07:43.749 --> 00:07:48.240
And of course, he also threw on a Human Head
Stress Ball - so he could simulate torturing

00:07:48.240 --> 00:07:51.430
a human head without actually needing to access
one.

00:07:51.430 --> 00:07:56.679
Dr. Bright was fascinated by the bizarre spectrum
of results so far, so he next moved onto the

00:07:56.679 --> 00:08:04.990
Amazon wishlist of SCP-076-2, also known as
Able - with whom Dr. Bright had a long-standing

00:08:04.990 --> 00:08:05.990
grudge.

00:08:05.990 --> 00:08:10.179
Able, upon resurrection, had to be restrained
by a massive amount of industrial-strength

00:08:10.179 --> 00:08:14.459
straps typically used to hold elephants in
place during surgery.

00:08:14.459 --> 00:08:18.819
After a solid 48 hours of relentless struggling,
they were finally able to get Able to dictate

00:08:18.819 --> 00:08:23.240
his wishlist picks to a Foundation researcher
at a nearby computer.

00:08:23.240 --> 00:08:29.330
His first pick was, predictably, a Chef'sChoice
Trizor XV EdgeSelect Professional Electric

00:08:29.330 --> 00:08:33.280
Knife Sharpener - for completely obvious reasons.

00:08:33.280 --> 00:08:37.590
He then added a book called “Weapons, a
Pictorial History” for some light reading

00:08:37.590 --> 00:08:39.600
around his favorite subject.

00:08:39.600 --> 00:08:44.210
And of course, he also added a vast collection
of Temporary Tattoos, because it’d been

00:08:44.210 --> 00:08:48.600
a while since he got his last badass battle
tat, but didn’t quite feel ready to commit

00:08:48.600 --> 00:08:51.190
to something at this time.

00:08:51.190 --> 00:08:57.460
Next came SCP-2273, Major Alexei Belitrov,
an alternate history’s Russian militant

00:08:57.460 --> 00:09:00.020
trapped in a biomechanical weapon of war.

00:09:00.020 --> 00:09:05.030
He first added an Oversized Bread Pillow,
which made him nostalgic for the Russian bread

00:09:05.030 --> 00:09:06.570
line days of his childhood.

00:09:06.570 --> 00:09:12.660
Next, he threw on a self-sustaining aquatic
ecosphere - After all, in his native timeline,

00:09:12.660 --> 00:09:15.480
almost all life was wiped out by nuclear war.

00:09:15.480 --> 00:09:20.910
It brought him great comfort to have a hand-held
reminder that life still goes on, somewhere.

00:09:20.910 --> 00:09:25.590
And also, he treated himself to a hand-held,
pump operated Fart Vacuum, cause whenever

00:09:25.590 --> 00:09:32.050
he farted in his exo-suit and was just stuck
in there with it, it was a truly hellish experience.

00:09:32.050 --> 00:09:37.750
Of course, Dr. Bright couldn’t resist trying
out his experiment with SCP-049, also known

00:09:37.750 --> 00:09:42.530
as The Plague Doctor - One of the SCP Foundation’s
more intellectual captives.

00:09:42.530 --> 00:09:48.310
Predictably, the first item added to his wish
list was a 157-piece set of stainless steel

00:09:48.310 --> 00:09:49.760
surgical tools.

00:09:49.760 --> 00:09:55.090
Next, in a fun twist of fate, the Plague Doctor
decided to add a realistic rubber pigeon mask

00:09:55.090 --> 00:09:56.590
to his wish list.

00:09:56.590 --> 00:10:01.450
When Dr. Bright later questioned him about
this, he said, “Well, dear boy, this is

00:10:01.450 --> 00:10:05.910
one of the only masks I’ve ever seen that
will actually fit over my head.”

00:10:05.910 --> 00:10:10.840
He then added an insulated tote bag with the
words “HUMAN ORGAN FOR TRANSPLANT” printed

00:10:10.840 --> 00:10:12.240
on the side.

00:10:12.240 --> 00:10:17.070
Funnily enough, he’d probably be the only
one to actually use it for human organs…

00:10:17.070 --> 00:10:21.050
Next came SCP-029, also known as the Daughter
of Shadows.

00:10:21.050 --> 00:10:25.670
Dr. Bright was particularly eager to include
this extremely violent Keter class in his

00:10:25.670 --> 00:10:30.430
new experiment, because she attempted escape
from containment constantly, so it would save

00:10:30.430 --> 00:10:32.780
a lot of money and lives to keep her pacified.

00:10:32.780 --> 00:10:37.210
When she stopped trying to strangle the staff
member giving her the iPhone, she first selected

00:10:37.210 --> 00:10:42.890
a Skull-Shaped Makeup Brush Holder, which
satisfied two of her greatest passions: Death

00:10:42.890 --> 00:10:44.170
and being adored.

00:10:44.170 --> 00:10:49.580
She also bought an instructional book called
“A Girl’s Guide to Femdom”, and a BluRay

00:10:49.580 --> 00:10:52.550
copy of Indiana Jones and the Temple of Doom.

00:10:52.550 --> 00:10:57.790
Next came the kind but haunted SCP-073, also
known as Cain.

00:10:57.790 --> 00:11:02.130
The first addition to his wishlist was a pair
of flip-flops with artificial grass along

00:11:02.130 --> 00:11:06.270
the sole, to simulate the sensation of walking
on grass.

00:11:06.270 --> 00:11:10.470
Seeing as Cain can’t walk on real grass
without killing it, these grassy sandals would

00:11:10.470 --> 00:11:12.720
naturally give him some comfort.

00:11:12.720 --> 00:11:17.680
Next, he added the instructional book Dancing
For Cats, so he could practice his dance moves

00:11:17.680 --> 00:11:22.420
with Josie the Half-Cat, aka SCP-529.

00:11:22.420 --> 00:11:28.290
And in a bizarre turn of events, he bought
30 pieces of fake garlic, so he could fantasize

00:11:28.290 --> 00:11:31.800
about touching garlic without it immediately
dying.

00:11:31.800 --> 00:11:38.110
Next came SCP-811, the sweet but simple-minded
humanoid amphibian known as the Swamp Woman.

00:11:38.110 --> 00:11:42.320
Would her Amazon wishlist also be esoterically
wholesome like the anomaly herself?

00:11:42.320 --> 00:11:48.850
First, she added a stuffed blobfish plush,
commenting “Aaaah, him so sweet” upon

00:11:48.850 --> 00:11:50.880
clicking “add to wishlist.”

00:11:50.880 --> 00:11:54.940
She next added a novelty yodeling pickle,
the mere idea of which brought her a great

00:11:54.940 --> 00:11:56.330
deal of amusement.

00:11:56.330 --> 00:12:01.590
She also bought a talking hamster toy, largely
due to being extremely lonely most days in

00:12:01.590 --> 00:12:06.280
containment, and even being willing to tolerate
a pretend rodent with a pre-set selection

00:12:06.280 --> 00:12:09.130
of phrases as a social substitute.

00:12:09.130 --> 00:12:14.060
Next came the disembodied brain of the mad
inventor Jeremy Valdez, also known to the

00:12:14.060 --> 00:12:17.250
SCP Foundation as SCP-2099.

00:12:17.250 --> 00:12:22.850
As soon as the terminally scatter-brained,
uh, brain, made up his mind, Dr. Bright reviewed

00:12:22.850 --> 00:12:23.850
the list.

00:12:23.850 --> 00:12:26.840
First, he added a book called The Brain That
Changes Itself.

00:12:26.840 --> 00:12:31.200
Next, he nabbed some pretty dubious brain
supplements that the Amazon reviews suggest

00:12:31.200 --> 00:12:33.540
give the user rather violent diarrhea.

00:12:33.540 --> 00:12:38.000
And, bafflingly, a Dill Pickle-flavored tube
of lip balm.

00:12:38.000 --> 00:12:41.950
No, we don’t know how Dr. Valdez intends
to use that, either.

00:12:41.950 --> 00:12:47.260
Dr. Bright also gave an iPhone locked onto
the Amazon app to Dr. Clef, who isn’t an

00:12:47.260 --> 00:12:52.260
anomaly contained by the SCP Foundation, but
was still bizarre and enigmatic enough that

00:12:52.260 --> 00:12:57.010
Dr. Bright was eager to psychoanalyze him
via his Amazon wishlist selections.

00:12:57.010 --> 00:13:01.930
First, he selected a book entitled “Shotguns:
A Comprehensive Guide.”

00:13:01.930 --> 00:13:05.790
Next, he threw on an extensive ukulele repair
kit.

00:13:05.790 --> 00:13:09.010
And of course, a Pooping Pooches Calendar.

00:13:09.010 --> 00:13:10.580
Stay classy, Dr. Clef.

00:13:10.580 --> 00:13:14.820
Dr. Bright performed this exact experiment
on hundreds of different anomalies, gaining

00:13:14.820 --> 00:13:19.420
what he believed to be invaluable data that
could help enrich the containment of so many

00:13:19.420 --> 00:13:23.330
of them, and thus, prevent countless containment
breaches.

00:13:23.330 --> 00:13:25.100
This had been such a brilliant plan!

00:13:25.100 --> 00:13:30.120
Dr. Bright felt beyond pleased with himself,
until he received an incredibly angry-sounding

00:13:30.120 --> 00:13:31.870
phone call from O5-1.

00:13:31.870 --> 00:13:38.330
“Jack, you overhyped piece of gaudy jewelry,
what the hell have you done this time!?”

00:13:38.330 --> 00:13:43.000
Dr. Bright was deeply confused as to why the
O5 Council were so angry at him.

00:13:43.000 --> 00:13:47.250
He’d been performing one of the most benign
experiments in the history of the SCP Foundation

00:13:47.250 --> 00:13:52.010
- He hadn’t even needed to kill a single
D-Class, so why all the vitriol?

00:13:52.010 --> 00:13:55.750
Moments later, though, Dr. Bright would discover
the horrifying error of his ways.

00:13:55.750 --> 00:14:00.540
You see, when Dr. Bright had given these hundreds
of anomalies iPhones with location services

00:14:00.540 --> 00:14:06.650
enabled and the Amazon app open, he’d also
forgotten to log out of his account - Which,

00:14:06.650 --> 00:14:11.380
incidentally, was also twinned to his SCP
Foundation expenses account.

00:14:11.380 --> 00:14:15.810
In essence, the various anomalies of the SCP
Foundation had ordered tens of thousands of

00:14:15.810 --> 00:14:20.640
dollars worth of the most bizarre Amazon products
imaginable - And they were heading straight

00:14:20.640 --> 00:14:21.640
for Site-19.

00:14:21.640 --> 00:14:26.810
The waste of money would be bad enough, but
the true threat was far more grave: This little

00:14:26.810 --> 00:14:31.240
mistake had exposed the location of the SCP
Foundation’s biggest containment site to

00:14:31.240 --> 00:14:33.620
one of its most powerful enemies.

00:14:33.620 --> 00:14:37.500
And by the looks of things, he was already
on his way.

00:14:37.500 --> 00:14:41.850
Not long after the fateful order was made,
a fleet of Amazon-branded attack helicopters

00:14:41.850 --> 00:14:47.740
choked the skies, while fleets of Amazon ground
vehicles approached Site-19 in tight formation.

00:14:47.740 --> 00:14:52.029
Mobile Task Forces and guards converged around
the perimeter of the site, hoping to fend

00:14:52.029 --> 00:14:56.740
off the coming attack, but even they were
overwhelmed by the sheer extent of the force

00:14:56.740 --> 00:14:57.910
coming towards them.

00:14:57.910 --> 00:15:02.470
Dr. Bright, horrified at the chaos he’d
wrought, ran to the front lines outside the

00:15:02.470 --> 00:15:05.750
site, ready to take on the new threat with
the Foundation’s finest.

00:15:05.750 --> 00:15:10.570
That’s when the line of vehicles came to
a stop, and the helicopters continued circling

00:15:10.570 --> 00:15:11.779
in the sky.

00:15:11.779 --> 00:15:15.920
Doors opened and heavily-armed soldiers in
tactical gear began to spill out.

00:15:15.920 --> 00:15:20.430
It was an army like nothing Dr. Bright or
the Foundation had ever seen before.

00:15:20.430 --> 00:15:25.570
The army split like the red sea, and their
great and terrible leader stepped forwards,

00:15:25.570 --> 00:15:29.980
muscles rippling out from the sleeves of his
overpriced Hugo Boss vest.

00:15:29.980 --> 00:15:31.390
It was him.

00:15:31.390 --> 00:15:38.850
CEO, entrepreneur, born in 1964 - Jeffrey,
Jeffrey Bezos.

00:15:38.850 --> 00:15:43.510
One of the world’s most ruthless businessmen,
with biceps that could bust skulls like he

00:15:43.510 --> 00:15:44.840
busted unions.

00:15:44.840 --> 00:15:49.430
And, little known fact, he’s a man with
close ties to the incredibly wealthy group

00:15:49.430 --> 00:15:52.730
of interest Marshall, Carter, and Dark Ltd.

00:15:52.730 --> 00:15:58.780
He was here for one reason and one reason
only: To acquire the SCP Foundation, and add

00:15:58.780 --> 00:16:02.310
it to his world-devouring conglomerate of
evil.

00:16:02.310 --> 00:16:06.330
“Don’t worry, Dr. Bright,” he said with
a devious smirk.

00:16:06.330 --> 00:16:08.590
“Not much will change under my management.

00:16:08.590 --> 00:16:12.070
I already treat my employees like D-Class
personnel.”

00:16:12.070 --> 00:16:20.730
But Dr. Bright would never see the SCP Foundation
taken over by such a heartless, self-interested

00:16:20.730 --> 00:16:22.400
Lex Luthor wannabe.

00:16:22.400 --> 00:16:27.960
He challenged Jeff Bezos to a one-on-one battle
to decide the fate of the SCP Foundation,

00:16:27.960 --> 00:16:32.050
seeing as it was his fault and his fault alone
that the location of Site-19 had been revealed

00:16:32.050 --> 00:16:34.680
to Bezos and his underpaid minions.

00:16:34.680 --> 00:16:38.310
Bezos agreed to the terms, and the battle
began.

00:16:38.310 --> 00:16:40.779
Dr. Bright put up his dukes and prepared to
tussle.

00:16:40.779 --> 00:16:46.810
Bezos, however, flexed his muscles so inhumanly
hard that his vest and shirt exploded into

00:16:46.810 --> 00:16:50.700
shreds, revealing his surprisingly swole physique
underneath.

00:16:50.700 --> 00:16:55.810
Little did Dr. Bright know, Bezos had augmented
his body with nanomachines to massively increase

00:16:55.810 --> 00:16:57.820
his strength and durability.

00:16:57.820 --> 00:17:01.380
He was now a combatant of truly anomalous
power.

00:17:01.380 --> 00:17:03.529
How would Dr. Bright possibly win?

00:17:03.529 --> 00:17:07.919
The two ran towards each other across the
battlefield outside of Site-19, both roaring

00:17:07.919 --> 00:17:12.470
in animal rage, ready for this clash of the
titans that would determine the fate of the

00:17:12.470 --> 00:17:14.339
SCP Foundation forever…

00:17:14.339 --> 00:17:18.999
“...Long story short,” Dr. Bright said
to Dr. Hayberg, who, at this point, was thoroughly

00:17:18.999 --> 00:17:19.999
confused.

00:17:19.999 --> 00:17:21.319
“Everything was okay in the end.

00:17:21.319 --> 00:17:22.319
See?

00:17:22.319 --> 00:17:25.360
It’s important not to dwell on our mistakes.”

00:17:25.360 --> 00:17:26.360
“Uh…

00:17:26.360 --> 00:17:31.909
Thanks, Dr. Bright, I guess?” he said, unsure
if anything Dr. Bright just said to him was

00:17:31.909 --> 00:17:36.830
true or if it was all some complex, free-associating
fantasy he made up on the spot.

00:17:36.830 --> 00:17:39.100
Dr. Bright gave him a playful punch in the
shoulder.

00:17:39.100 --> 00:17:40.919
“Any time, Rookie.

00:17:40.919 --> 00:17:41.919
Any time.”

00:17:41.919 --> 00:17:47.450
Now go check out “SCP-963 What Would You
Do If You Were Immortal Like Dr. Jack Bright?”

00:17:47.450 --> 00:17:48.450
and “DR.

00:17:48.450 --> 00:17:54.309
BRIGHT FOR PRESIDENT SCP-4444 - Bush v. Gore”
for more wacky misadventures from the immortally

00:17:54.309 --> 00:17:55.079
silly Dr. Bright!

